# Rust style

Prefer explicit, boring Rust. Avoid extra generics, macros, `unsafe`, `clone`, `Arc`, `Mutex`, `RwLock`, and trait objects unless required now.

```rust
// ❌ BAD — framework and I/O inside a service
pub async fn hello() -> String {
    println!("hello");
    panic!("missing config");
}

// ✅ GOOD — pure service, caller maps to HTTP/HTML
pub fn hello(&self) -> Greeting {
    Greeting::new(format!("Hello from {APP_NAME}"))
}
```

- Use `Result`. Do not `panic!` / `unwrap` on normal application paths.
- Keep `AppError` internal. Map user-facing HTTP/HTML in the app crate, not in `core`.
- Logging: `tracing` only. No `println!` or `dbg!` in apps. Initialize tracing once in `main`.
- Tests should assert behavior (config, health, greeting, rendered HTML), not line coverage.
